; LS120MP.INF  Parallel-port LS-120 (Shuttle EPAT) miniport, IBM 5160 + Inboard
;
; https://github.com/Mike1978uk/win95-intel-inboard-386pc  (issue #22)
;
; *** FIRST BUILD WITH A WORKING TRANSPORT. Never yet run under Windows. ***
;
; It registers with SCSIPORT, takes its parallel-port base from the Settings
; tab, brings the drive up (see below) and serves it as an ATAPI removable.
;
; The bring-up is the part that took two sessions to find: the drive powers up
; HELD IN ATA RESET, so until SRST is pulsed on device control every task-file
; register reads 00h and the hardware is indistinguishable from an absent
; bridge. That reset is not in epat.c - it lives one layer up, in Linux's
; pcd.c. Proven cold on the real 5160 on 2026-09-10 with Imation's
; SD120PPD.SYS REM'd out: the task file returns the ATAPI signature 14 EB.
; Full account: drivers/imation_ls120/TRANSPORT_SPEC.md section 5.
;
; What is NOT yet proven: any of it under Windows. The keyboard question below
; is still open, and the block data path is a nibble byte-loop - correct, and
; slow. Treat a first install as a test, not a deployment.
;
; Derived from XTIDEMP.INF, which is the INF proven to install and load a
; polled, no-IRQ miniport on this exact machine. Differences, and why:
;
;   1. IOConfig     378-37F and the other two standard LPT bases. Eight ports,
;                   because an EPP-capable port decodes base+0..base+7 and a
;                   later phase will need them. Phase 0 reads only base+1.
;   2. PortDriver   LS120MP.MPD.
;   3. No IRQConfig, no DMAConfig. The DOS build offers both IRQ 7 and polled
;                   mode (`/di`); we poll, and declare no interrupt handler at
;                   all, so SCSIPORT is never asked to connect one.
;
; PREREQUISITE, not optional: inbrdpc.sys must appear in [SafeList] in
; WINDOWS\IOS.INI or IOS declines every miniport on this machine. Issue #17,
; and docs/ios_safelist_howto.md.
;
; BEFORE INSTALLING: if Imation's own SD120PPD.MPD is installed, remove its
; node in Device Manager first. Two miniports claiming the same LPT base will
; conflict, and leaving Imation's loaded would confound the entire test - it is
; the thing under suspicion.


[Version]
signature="$CHICAGO$"
Class=SCSIAdapter
Provider=%MFG%
LayoutFile=LAYOUT.INF

[Manufacturer]
%MFG%=LS120


[LS120]
%LS120PP%=LS120_ISA


[LS120_ISA]
LogConfig=*LS120MP.LogConfig
CopyFiles=@LS120MP.MPD
AddReg=IOS, LS120_REG, Poll


[LS120_REG]
HKR,,PortDriver,,LS120MP.MPD

; Which parallel port the drive is on. Editable in Device Manager -> the
; controller -> Settings, and handed to HwFindAdapter as ArgumentString. This
; is the convention Imation's own README documents for machines where the
; driver cannot work the address out for itself - and on this machine the
; owner already knows it, because CONFIG.SYS passes /port:378 to the DOS
; driver. Asking beats probing, and probing is what we are avoiding.
; MODE= picks the data transport, and is optional.
;
;   MODE=SPP   nibble. Works on ANY parallel port. ~4 port accesses per
;              byte, which on this machine is ~4 x 3.9 us of bus sync.
;   MODE=ECP   the ECP FIFO at base+400h. ~1 access per byte. Needs a
;              port with a real ECR at base+402h.
;   omitted    probe for an ECR and use ECP if one answers, else SPP.
;
; Auto-detection is the default and is right on every port seen so far,
; but a probe can be wrong in either direction on hardware nobody here
; has tested, so both answers are forceable: pin SPP if a port claims an
; ECR it does not honour, pin ECP if a working ECR fails the probe.
; The vendor DOS driver selects ECP Read / ECP Write on this machine
; (captured live, TRANSPORT_SPEC.md section 0), so ECP is the expected
; outcome here.
; SHIPPED DEFAULT IS AUTO-DETECT - MODE= is deliberately absent below.
; Forcing ECP here would mean a wrong probe result could not fall back,
; and the ECP path is new. On a card with a real ECR the probe selects
; ECP by itself, so auto costs nothing on this machine and keeps the
; safety net everywhere else. Add MODE= by hand if the probe is wrong.
HKR,,AdapterSettings,,"PORT=0x378"

; Polling, not an interrupt. See the note in DriverEntry: a NULL interrupt
; handler with a zero level says "we poll" without asking SCSIPORT to connect
; anything.
[Poll]
HKR,,Polling,,1


[*LS120MP.Det]
AddReg=ForceMPCopy


; The three standard LPT bases. Several ranges on purpose: the driver takes
; its base from AdapterSettings and does not care what the node is assigned,
; and narrowing an INF to one range is what produced a resource-conflict
; callout, a manually forced configuration and a run of wedged shutdowns on
; 2026-09-05 (technique 97). Do not narrow this.
[*LS120MP.LogConfig]
ConfigPriority=HARDRECONFIG
IOConfig=378-37F(3FF::), 278-27F(3FF::), 3BC-3BF(3FF::)


[DestinationDirs]
DefaultDestDir=12

[SourceDisksFiles]
LS120MP.MPD = 1

[SourceDisksNames]
1 = "LS-120 Miniport Installation Disk",,

[IOS]
HKR,,DevLoader,,*IOS
HKR,,DontLoadIfConflict,,Y

[ForceMPCopy]
HKR,,NoSetupUI,,1


[Strings]
MFG="win95-intel-inboard-386pc"
LS120PP="Parallel-port LS-120 (Shuttle EPAT, polled)"
SCSIClassName="SCSI controllers"
